 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Draw two (or three) overlapping circles inside a rectangle (universal set)",
   "Fill the intersection first",
   "Work outwards: subtract intersection from each circle total",
   "The remainder goes outside all circles",
   "For conditional probability: restrict to the condition and find the proportion"
  ],
  "title": "How to Use Venn Diagrams & Conditional Probability",
  "content": "<p>A <strong>Venn diagram</strong> shows sets and their overlaps. Key notation: \\(A \\cap B\\) (intersection — both), \\(A \\cup B\\) (union — either or both), \\(A'\\) (complement — not A).</p><p><strong>Conditional probability:</strong> \\(P(A|B) = \\frac{P(A \\cap B)}{P(B)}\\) — the probability of A given that B has happened.</p><p>Fill Venn diagrams from the <strong>intersection first</strong>, then work outwards. The total of all regions must equal the total number of items.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>30 students: 18 play football (F), 12 play tennis (T), 7 play both. Find P(F|T).</strong></p><p>P(F|T) = \\(\\frac{7}{12}\\) (of the 12 tennis players, 7 also play football).</p>"
 },
